meta {
  name: reject-order
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/transition
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "status": "rejected",
    "reason": "We have run out of paneer this evening"
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses to reject an order that is already served", function () {
    // 409 is the CORRECT answer at this point in a sequential run: the four transitions above have
    // driven this order to `served`, and the state machine has no edge from there to `rejected`.
    // Asserting the refusal rather than tolerating it is what makes this a test.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(409)
  })

  test("and names the reason it refused", function () {
    expect(res.body.code).to.equal("TX_ORD_006")
  })
}

docs {
  > In a sequential run this **asserts the 409**, because the order has already been served by the
> requests above. To see it succeed, place a fresh order and point `order_uid` at that.

  # Reject an order

**Remove `reason` and this answers `TX_ORD_015` (422).** Reject and staff-cancel are the two
transitions a diner experiences as their order disappearing, so they must carry an explanation the
diner can be shown.

Only legal from `placed`. Run it against a fresh order, not one already accepted.
}
